WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.540
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 67 for June 10th, 2026.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.900
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.940
Picture this. Your phone suddenly becomes a pocket interpreter that jumps into any conversation

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:24.540
without missing a beat. That is exactly what happened today with a new tool from Google called

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:32.700
Gemini 3.5 Live Translate. It handles live speech-to-speech translation across more than 70 languages,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.320
starting the moment you speak and keeping the flow natural. Think of it like having a super

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.060
fast interpreter in your pocket who listens to one person grabs the meaning instantly and speaks

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:49.480
the translation in another language while the first person is still talking. The model makes quick

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:55.740
choices about speed versus accuracy, so the back-and-forth feels smooth instead of choppy, almost

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.000
like a skilled juggler keeping three balls in the air without dropping any. It also keeps the

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.360
speaker's tone, pitch, and rhythm, so the translated voice still sounds like the original person,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:13.340
not some flat robot. This matters because language barriers start to disappear in everyday moments,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.620
whether you are chatting with a friend from another country on a video call or helping a family member

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:23.960
at the doctor's office. Students could practice conversations with people around the world without

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:30.060
waiting for subtitles or slow apps, which is pretty wild when you think about it. For you personally,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:36.040
this could change how you connect with people online or travel more confidently, since the model stays

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:41.800
only a few seconds behind the speaker. You can try it right now in the Google Translate app on iOS

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:48.520
or Android. Open the app, choose the conversation mode, pick two languages, and start speaking to watch

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:55.080
the translation stream in real-time as you talk. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.680
where we look under the hood. You know how when you are texting a friend, your phone sometimes guesses

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:06.660
the next word before you finish typing? Now imagine that same auto-complete has to do three things at

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:12.140
once. Listen to what someone is saying right now, figure out the meaning in another language,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.700
and start speaking the translation while the first person is still talking. It also has to remember

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.520
the tone of voice so the translation does not sound robotic, like a musician who keeps the original

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.420
songs feeling while switching instruments. The model juggles these jobs by making tiny decisions every

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.780
fraction of a second about whether to wait a little longer for better accuracy, or to speak sooner

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.320
for a more natural flow. Similar to a relay runner deciding exactly when to pass the baton.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.160
It keeps a running memory of the conversation, so later sentences make sense with earlier ones,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.640
just like how you remember the start of a story when someone adds the ending, and that is basically

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:58.760
what Gemini 3.5 live translate is doing when it translates speech in real-time without awkward pauses.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:05.100
So next time someone says, real-time speech to speech translation, you can tell them it is basically

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:11.440
supercharged auto-complete that listens, thanks, and talks at the same time. Not so scary, right?

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.220
Let's move to some cool stuff you can try yourself. First up,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.900
you can make pictures using only emoji with tools like chat GPT. This works because the AI reads the

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.780
emoji as a creative prompt and fills in the scene with its own imagination, like turning a handful of

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.660
puzzle pieces into a full picture. It is a fun, low pressure way to experiment with image generation

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:40.180
without writing long descriptions. Anyone with a free account can try it. Go to the chat GPT website,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:46.520
start a new chat, and type something like a cat sleeping in a house under the sun, using those

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:53.100
emoji symbols. See what scene it creates, then mix different emoji to tell a tiny story, and notice

00:03:53.100 --> 00:04:00.800
how the AI adds details you did not expect. Next, Google is using its Gemini tool to help the Argentine

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.660
national team during the World Cup, turning the event into a showcase for AI. The system can analyze

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:14.080
game data, suggest strategies, or create fan content in real time, acting like an extra coach who

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.840
studies every play. It is a cool example of AI moving from phones into big live events. You can

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:27.140
explore similar Gemini features yourself by opening the Gemini app or the Gemini website and asking

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:33.640
it to analyze a sports clip or predict outcomes from public stats. Now for quick bits, starting with

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:39.300
the financial experiment. Someone launched a test eight months ago by handing several AI models

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.720
real cash along with live stock market feeds. The aim was to track how those models would manage

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:52.340
investments across many months under actual conditions. Five of the models have now outperformed the

00:04:52.340 --> 00:05:00.240
S&amp;P 500 benchmark. GPT delivered particularly strong performance within the group. The project supplies

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:06.080
a direct ongoing check on whether AI systems can make sound financial choices when given real

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:12.540
resources and continuous data. Observers can watch how the models react to market shifts without any

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:18.160
simulated environments involved. The results continue to update as the experiment runs forward.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.160
In another development, Germany's National Security Council approved a new institute called

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:31.720
DEAISI to test powerful AI models for risks. The institute follows the structure of an existing

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:38.240
group already operating in the UK. Its stated purpose is to examine models released by companies

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:45.500
such as Anthropic and OpenAI prior to broad public release. Officials want to identify potential

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:51.620
issues while the systems remain under control devaluation. The move reflects a wider pattern of

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.760
governments treating advanced AI with the same scrutiny applied to other high impact technologies.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.560
DEAISI will focus on safety assessments that check for harmful behaviors before deployment at scale.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.740
This approach mirrors regulatory steps taken with sectors like aviation and pharmaceuticals.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.380
The approval marks Germany's formal entry into dedicated AI oversight infrastructure.

